A fairly general solution to the problem of "protecting" those who
want to be protected from unsuitable content is for an internet access
provider to sell (as a value-added service), net access thru a caching 
http proxy (and/or SOCKS) server that provides "censored" access
to the net via a list of "good" or "bad" sites. A firewall can block
conventional access. This requries little or no change to clients
and cannot be bypassed with other client software (unlike many
proposed client/html hacks.)

It also requires no cooperation from the Internet as a whole, no
censorship of ordinary access, and works for many definitions
of "good" and "bad".

This is feasible today, if anyone cares to do the work and thinks there
is a real market for censorship (strange how most would-be censors want
someone else to assume the costs....)

