In <3q28b1$4c8@crcnis3.unl.edu> harbison@unlinfo.unl.edu (gerry harbison) writes:

>rohan@cs.monash.edu.au (Rohan Baxter) writes:

>>Carl Kadie has listed some perceived dangers with the 
>>labeling of documents proposals. Most of the dangers can be
>>avoided, provided:

>>1. the labeling scheme is truly voluntary 
>>(no one said "all" materials "have" to be labelled.
>>Carl doesn't have to label his pages. His pages have no label 
>>and are hence labeled "unlabeled" by browsers equipped to use 
>>labels :-) (Hope he doesn't mind!)  

>Well, exactly. He should mind.  There is a tradition in both our legal 
>systems that innocence be presumed until guilt is established.  

Since the labeling scheme is "truly voluntary", there are no
legal sanctions to worry about. I would imagine the vast
majority of pages would be unlabelled and no one would care.

>Labelling schemes ask web page authors to certify the 'innocence' of 
>their pages before the fact.  Would you ask the author of every magazine 
>article to include a statement at the beginning of his/her article to 
>tell the reader whether he/she might be offended by reading it?

No, because the vast majority of magazine articles do not need
labelling, of a voluntary nature or not. However, if the article was 
about a "sexual adventure involving some kids, an electric drill and 
lots of blood" (and lets face it, only a miniscule proportion of
magazine articles are on this topic), I would hope the author or 
publisher might signal the potentially offensive nature of the material 
somewhere along the line...

>The issue seems to me to be simple. There are two groups of people; 
>those who think the availability of sexual (or other) 
>material on web is a major concern, and those who do not.  Those who 
>do have an obvious solution; figure out individually or collectively 
>where the material that bothers them is, and act so that the children,
>(or even adults ) for whom they are custodians don't get to see what's on
>this index prohibitorum (if that's legal, of course; Carl's point 
>about the legality of school administrators deciding what kids in 
>their school should read is well taken).  They could buy SurfWatch, or 
>push Netscape to include a lock-out option, or even write their own 
>internet browser. 

I agree with all of this. The hypothesis is that voluntary labelling by 
authors may assist make the vetting process less ad hoc and
more automatic and without requiring a complete lock-out. 
I'm "hoping and suggesting" the up-side to voluntary labelling is
greater than the downside. 
(The analogy is like this. Say you take your kids on a shopping expedition.
As a good parent, you supervise where they go in the shopping mall.
Sure there are some potentially offensive materials in that Adult
Bookstores, in that video store and in that newsagency, but physical
"society" is structured to protect kids from those, even if they
do wander a little from your watch. Cyber "society" can help
do the same with its adult pages, etc.)

>It appears to me that rather than go
>to the trouble of doing this, they want the other group of people - 
>those of us who aren't concerned about this issue  - to do their work for 
>them. If nothing else, this seems the height of laziness.

Here we go again (as Carl says): Its "voluntary labelling". 
You don't have to do anything. I apologise for the work you incurred in 
responding to my post, but I do appreciate it :-). 

